  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n00Z upper air analysis this evening shows an open wave trough\nextending from the Upper Midwest back through portions of the\nCentral and Southern Plains. An 80kt sub-tropical jet is\ntransporting a plume of tropical moisture from the Pacific Ocean\nup into the Southern Plains and Lower Mississippi Valley this\nevening. A few isolated rain showers occurred late this afternoon\ninto around sunset across portions of southwest Tennessee and\nnorth Mississippi. Regional WSR-88D radar trends show rain\nshowers bisecting Arkansas late this evening. Warm and mild\nconditions persist late this evening with temperatures ranging\nfrom the 70s to lower 80s.\n\nLatest mesoanalysis currently shows a relatively stable\natmosphere in place across the region. Short-term model trends\nindicate this upstream activity will move into the Mid-South\novernight into Friday morning. Elevated instability will increase\nmodestly towards sunrise to possibly produce a thunderstorm as\nMLCAPE values increase to around 500 J/kg. This activity is\nexpected to be somewhat disorganized as better upper-level\nsupport will depart Friday morning. The 00Z HREF indicates\nprobabilities of surface-based CAPE values greater than 1000 J/kg\nare between 40-45 percent from late morning through early\nafternoon across portions of southwest Tennessee and northwest\nMississippi. This may occur if there are some breaks in the cloud\ncover. Otherwise, confidence in strong to severe thunderstorms\nremains marginal at best given the less than ideal shear and\ninstability. Precipitable water values will be approaching the\n99th percentile around 2 inches, normally favorable for heavy\nrainfall. However, confidence in overall convective coverage, a\nlack of upper-level support, and dry antecedent conditions will\nmitigate any overall flooding threat. Current forecast rainfall\namounts for the entire Mid-South have trended down again with\ntotals averaging around one inch areawide for the entire weekend.\n\nShort-term model trends suggest the weak cold front will stall\nacross the region Friday with a minimum in showers and\nthunderstorms Friday night into Saturday morning until a\nsecondary wave pushes the front completely through the area by\nSunday. A reinforcing cold front Sunday night will bring much\ncooler air into the region for early next week. Lows will range\nfrom the upper 40s to middle 50s with NBM probabilities of low\ntemperatures below 48 degrees averaging around 30-35 percent\nalong the MO/KY border Monday night. Highs next week will be near\nnormal with temperatures in the 70s to lower 80s.",
